WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:16.049
Porfirio Diaz: Mexico’s Gentleman Dictator

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:25.620
(Author: Morris M.)

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.490
In November 1910, Mexico exploded.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:31.630
Triggered by a rigged election, the Mexican
Revolution shook the nation.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:35.980
You’ve probably heard of its most-famous
sons: Emiliano Zapata and Pancho Villa.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:42.100
You probably even know its biggest set-pieces,
such as the 1914 US occupation of Veracruz.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:46.250
But how much do most of us know about the
guy who started it all?

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:52.220
About the guy whose rule convinced hundreds
of thousands of Mexicans to take up arms and

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:54.350
fight to overthrow him?

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:59.690
Born in 1830, Porfirio Diaz began life as
a poor nobody before rising to become his

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:02.270
country’s absolute dictator.

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:08.299
Starting in 1876, Diaz ruled Mexico with an
iron fist for over three and a half decades,

00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:12.820
leaving power only when swept from it by the
tides of revolution.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:16.930
Yet the story of Diaz is more than just the
story of an autocrat.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:21.490
Although a strongman, Diaz oversaw one of
the most-sustained periods of progress in

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:22.700
Mexican history.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:28.000
Under his watch, the country modernized and
transformed… even as democracy was trampled

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.000
into the dust.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.821
A devil to some, an angel to others, this
is the life of Porfirio Diaz: Mexico’s gentleman

00:01:34.821 --> 00:01:37.140
dictator.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:41.800
Chaos Nation

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:46.640
If you were a gambler, the odds you’d have
given on newborn Porfirio Diaz one day ruling

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.290
Mexico would’ve been somewhere in the region
of several quintillion to one.

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:56.470
When Diaz was born on September 15, 1830,
it was to dirt-poor mestizo parents in the

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:57.780
state of Oaxaca.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.810
If you’re not up on your terminology, “mestizo”
means mixed-race.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:06.670
Diaz’s ancestors had been a combination
of white Spaniards and indigenous Mixtec people.

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:10.950
But while that might sound interesting today,
in early Mexico it was pretty much a guarantee

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:13.630
that you were gonna spend your life wallowing
in poverty.

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:14.630
And wallow the Diazes did.

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:19.590
When he was just three or five, Diaz’s carpenter
father died, leaving the family penniless.

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.730
The family was so poor that they couldn’t
afford shoes.

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:27.380
There are tales that, as a child, Diaz spied
on a local cobbler, trying to figure out how

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:30.090
to make shoes for his family himself.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:33.920
But then life wasn’t a picnic for anybody
in 1830s’ Mexico.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:38.640
The reason being that the country was just
embarking on a solid half-century of chaos.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.360
OK, so, in order for us all to really understand
what’s going on, we need to take a quick

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.470
stroll through Mexican history.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:49.800
Until only a decade before Porfirio Diaz’s
birth, Mexico had been the Viceroyalty of

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.200
New Spain, an outpost of the Spanish empire.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:57.570
But that empire had lost its jewel in 1821,
when New Spain had won independence and declared

00:02:57.570 --> 00:02:58.760
itself Mexico.

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:03.050
The early years of Mexico had been kinda like
watching a confused middle schooler, trying

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:06.370
on various personalities to see what sticks.

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:09.410
It had been an empire, been a republic.

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:14.660
By the time Porfirio Diaz was old enough to
be spying on shoemakers, though, it had settled

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:17.910
into a much-less welcome form.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:20.020
Mexico was a mess.

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:23.300
The trouble had begun with 1828’s disputed
election.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:28.850
What started as a democratic wobble soon became
the norm, until Mexico was like a big plate

00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:30.599
of political Jell-o.

00:03:30.599 --> 00:03:37.190
To give you some idea just how shaky things
were; between 1828 and 1857, the Mexican presidency

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:39.909
changed hands fifty times.

00:03:39.909 --> 00:03:43.670
This chaos was helped by a number of factors,
both internal and external.

00:03:43.670 --> 00:03:48.090
Externally, there were all the European powers
who kept invading, like the Spanish in 1829,

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:49.690
or the French in 1838.

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:54.370
Internally, there was the fact that Mexico’s
liberals and conservatives would rather focus

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:59.130
on dicking one another over than actually
governing the country.

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:00.739
Then there was Santa Anna.

00:04:00.739 --> 00:04:03.670
You might recognize Santa Anna from your American
history class.

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:09.440
He’s the Mexican dictator who laid siege
to the Alamo and lost Texas to a bunch of

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:10.440
revolutionaries.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:13.299
But he was also Mexico’s great political
survivor.

00:04:13.299 --> 00:04:17.759
Flitting between left and right, he tumbled
in and out of the presidency all the way from

00:04:17.759 --> 00:04:23.590
1833 to 1855, adding to the general sense
of chaos.

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:28.750
But if the decade of Porfirio Diaz’s childhood
was characterized by a Mexico constantly on

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:33.379
the brink of crisis, it had nothing on what
was gonna come next.

00:04:33.379 --> 00:04:39.280
The 1840s would see the start of a period
when Mexico was more or less constantly on

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:40.280
fire.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:45.270
And, when the entire country was nothing but
ashes, Porfirio Diaz would be the only person

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:47.469
left standing.

00:04:47.469 --> 00:04:50.849
The Inferno

00:04:50.849 --> 00:04:56.210
There’s a parallel universe where typing
“Porfirio Diaz” into Google will get you

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:59.780
nothing but a list of obscure Mexican priests.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:01.689
Not in this universe, though.

00:05:01.689 --> 00:05:07.180
In this universe, Diaz’s teenage attempts
to join a seminary were interrupted by war.

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:12.430
On 25 April, 1846, a group of American soldiers
deliberately wandered into the disputed zone

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:16.159
between Mexico and the newly-annexed state
of Texas.

00:05:16.159 --> 00:05:21.710
When the Mexican border guards opened fire,
the US used it as a pretext to launch a full-scale

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:22.710
war.

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:30.330
If Mexico in 1846 was a wobbly stack of Jell-o,
then the Mexican-American War was like a hurricane

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:33.120
blowing the entire pantry away.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.099
The American invasion caused the government
to collapse, allowing Santa Anna to seize

00:05:37.099 --> 00:05:40.039
the presidency for the ninth time.

00:05:40.039 --> 00:05:45.199
Down in Oaxaca, 15-year old Porfirio Diaz
abandoned the priesthood and instead enlisted

00:05:45.199 --> 00:05:48.620
in a student militia to fight for Mexico’s
honor.

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:51.599
But this wasn’t the war Diaz was destined
to shine in.

00:05:51.599 --> 00:05:57.069
Heck, he wouldn’t even become the biggest
name from his home in Oaxaca.

00:05:57.069 --> 00:06:00.000
That honor would fall to Benito Juarez.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.969
Born in 1806, Juarez was from a fully indigenous
family - unlike the mestizo Diaz - and hadn’t

00:06:05.969 --> 00:06:07.740
spoken Spanish until he was 12.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:13.039
He was, however, ferociously intelligent,
and had become a lawyer and leading liberal

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:14.189
in Oaxaca.

00:06:14.189 --> 00:06:19.669
Come 1846, Juarez had been elected state governor,
just in time for the Mexican-American War.

00:06:19.669 --> 00:06:24.949
When it became clear that Mexico was destined
to lose, Juarez made a stand and refused to

00:06:24.949 --> 00:06:29.430
send any more young Oaxacan men to die in
Santa Anna’s army.

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:34.460
The result was Juarez landing a place on both
Oaxaca’s unofficial list of Liberal heroes,

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:39.889
and Santa Anna’s very official list of “Guys
I’m Totally Gonna Kill.”

00:06:39.889 --> 00:06:46.469
On February 2, 1848, the war ended, and Porfirio
Diaz demobilized from his student militia.

00:06:46.469 --> 00:06:50.770
But rather than return to the priesthood,
he instead returned to Oaxaca, determined

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:53.770
to follow in Benito Juarez’s liberal footsteps.

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:59.349
Today, Diaz is associated with Mexico’s
conservatives, but he started out learning

00:06:59.349 --> 00:07:00.970
at Juarez’s feet.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:07.849
This gave Diaz a front row seat for Santa
Anna’s next vindictive move - and, yes,

00:07:07.849 --> 00:07:10.020
emphasis fully on the dick.

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:14.969
In 1853, Santa Anna returned to the presidency
for the first time since the war.

00:07:14.969 --> 00:07:21.469
Only this time, he styled himself “His Serene
Highness,” and launched a brutal consolidation

00:07:21.469 --> 00:07:22.469
of power.

00:07:22.469 --> 00:07:26.749
A consolidation that included kicking Benito
Juarez out the country.

00:07:26.749 --> 00:07:30.889
When Diaz heard his mentor was being forced
into exile, he was livid.

00:07:30.889 --> 00:07:35.029
He marched down to the town square where a
referendum was taking place on Santa Anna’s

00:07:35.029 --> 00:07:40.169
coup - watched over by Santa Anna’s armed
soldiers, naturally - and openly cast a vote

00:07:40.169 --> 00:07:41.959
against the dictator.

00:07:41.959 --> 00:07:45.819
In the chaos that followed, Diaz then managed
to mount a horse, steal a gun, and shoot his

00:07:45.819 --> 00:07:49.989
way out the town and off into the mountains
to foment rebellion.

00:07:49.989 --> 00:07:55.270
It was both the most-Mexican exit we’ve
ever heard, and a piece of excellent political

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:56.270
timing.

00:07:56.270 --> 00:08:00.569
Santa Anna’s new regime was as unpopular
as a president returning for the eleventh

00:08:00.569 --> 00:08:02.459
time tends to be.

00:08:02.459 --> 00:08:06.319
When others took up arms against him, it was
clear his time was over.

00:08:06.319 --> 00:08:09.550
In 1855, Santa Anna resigned and fled into
exile.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:13.689
He would die in poverty in 1876, having never
regained the presidency.

00:08:13.689 --> 00:08:18.929
In the aftermath of Santa Anna’s two decade
hold on politics collapsing, the Liberals

00:08:18.929 --> 00:08:19.929
took power.

00:08:19.929 --> 00:08:25.559
Juarez came back to Mexico, Diaz came back
down out the mountains, and everyone cheered.

00:08:25.559 --> 00:08:27.360
It should’ve been a moment of calm.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:32.089
A hard-earned bit of breathing space after
decades of chaos.

00:08:32.089 --> 00:08:35.860
But this is 19th Century Mexico we’re talking
about.

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:41.820
And if there’s one thing 19th Century Mexico
was all about, it was chaos.

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:46.800
A Warrior’s Rise

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:50.550
When the Liberals took power in the wake of
Santa Anna’s fall, they weren’t in the

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:51.560
mood for compromise.

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:56.310
They promulgated a new, hyper-liberal constitution
that not only guaranteed free speech and the

00:08:56.310 --> 00:09:01.270
right to bear arms, but also stripped the
Catholic Church of its power and property.

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:05.360
Unfortunately for Diaz and Juarez, Mexico
had a lot of Catholics.

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:10.100
A lot of Conservative Catholics now sure that
this Liberal government was destroying their

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:11.170
way of life.

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:17.089
And so we come to war #3,792 of this video:
the Reform War.

00:09:17.089 --> 00:09:19.860
Trust us, it won’t be the last.

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:25.149
Pitting Liberals against Conservatives in
a gigantic bloodletting, the Reform War - or

00:09:25.149 --> 00:09:28.430
La Reforma - sucked for just about everyone.

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:31.210
But not for Porfirio Diaz.

00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:32.970
Well, it did physically.

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:36.960
In one early battle he got shot, leaving a
festering wound in his side that wouldn’t

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:39.610
heal for two whole years.

00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:40.610
Yuck.

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:43.769
But, politically, the war paid dividends.

00:09:43.769 --> 00:09:49.720
When La Reforma ended in 1860 with a Liberal
victory, Diaz was promoted for his heroism.

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:54.280
That meant he became a general just in time
for the French invasion of Mexico.

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:58.060
Yep, its more war, this time courtesy of our
old friend, Napoleon III.

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:01.819
After winning the Reform War, Benito Juarez
had retaken the presidency and discovered

00:10:01.819 --> 00:10:03.529
the country was broke.

00:10:03.529 --> 00:10:06.510
So he suspended all repayments of foreign
debts.

00:10:06.510 --> 00:10:11.620
However, one of those foreign debts just happened
to be held by France, now under the dictatorship

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:14.670
of Napoleon’s nephew, Louis-Napoleon.

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:19.790
And if Napoleon III had inherited one thing
from his uncle, it was his love of invading

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:21.430
foreign nations.

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:26.319
We don’t have time to go into the French
invasion in detail - which is a shame as its

00:10:26.319 --> 00:10:32.370
super interesting, and involves Louis-Napoleon
proclaiming a bewhiskered Austrian named Maximilian

00:10:32.370 --> 00:10:34.079
Emperor of Mexico.

00:10:34.079 --> 00:10:38.160
If you want to learn more, check out our video
on Maximilian.

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:43.139
For now, though, we’re gonna touch on one
key date you’ve definitely heard of: Cinco

00:10:43.139 --> 00:10:45.550
de Mayo.

00:10:45.550 --> 00:10:53.380
If you live north of the border, there’s
a good chance you only know Cinco de Mayo

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:58.360
as the day frat boys don sombreros and get
hammered on tequila.

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:01.970
But it was also the date of the Battle of
Puebla.

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:07.230
On May 5, 1862, the Mexican Army faced the
French in a battle where they were outnumbered

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:08.260
3 to 1.

00:11:08.260 --> 00:11:11.800
General Porfirio Diaz was technically second
in command of the Mexicans.

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:16.589
But, really, it was thanks to him that we
celebrate Cinco de Mayo at all.

00:11:16.589 --> 00:11:21.769
At the climax of the fighting, Diaz single-handedly
led a cavalry charge against the French.

00:11:21.769 --> 00:11:26.189
So daring was his attack that the French lines
broke, resulting in a Mexican victory.

00:11:26.189 --> 00:11:31.350
The Battle of Puebla raised Porfirio Diaz
from being a mere Liberal icon to a folk hero

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:32.430
for the entire nation.

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:37.430
It helped that Diaz followed his win up with
escapades like getting captured and breaking

00:11:37.430 --> 00:11:39.100
out of prison twice.

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:44.139
By the time the French abandoned their Mexican
adventure, Porfirio Diaz was to Mexico what

00:11:44.139 --> 00:11:46.329
Davy Crockett is to America.

00:11:46.329 --> 00:11:47.800
And he knew this.

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:53.329
When the post-invasion election of 1867 was
held, Diaz threw his hat into the ring.

00:11:53.329 --> 00:11:55.740
He probably should’ve won.

00:11:55.740 --> 00:11:59.310
The only reason he didn’t is that Benito
Juarez also ran.

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:03.930
While Diaz might’ve been a folk hero, Juarez
was the nation’s wartime leader.

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:07.749
It was like FDR asking the American public
for his fourth term.

00:12:07.749 --> 00:12:09.459
Of course Juarez was gonna win.

00:12:09.459 --> 00:12:10.910
So Diaz backed down.

00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:14.279
He stepped out the race and retired to a ranch
in Oaxaca.

00:12:14.279 --> 00:12:16.170
But here’s the thing.

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:19.180
Porfirio Diaz in 1867 wasn’t retirement
age.

00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:20.829
He wasn’t even 40.

00:12:20.829 --> 00:12:22.670
More than that, he was ambitious.

00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:25.529
More ambitious than Benito Juarez realized.

00:12:25.529 --> 00:12:45.470
In less than a decade, that ambition was gonna

00:12:45.470 --> 00:13:25.550
take him from “Mexican folk hero” to “bigger
dictator than Santa Anna.”

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:46.379
“No Reelection!”

00:13:46.379 --> 00:13:50.769
If we’ve done our job, by now it should
be clear that Mexico in the mid-19th Century

00:13:50.769 --> 00:13:52.329
was near-ungovernable.

00:13:52.329 --> 00:13:57.470
The entire period from 1828 onwards had been
nothing but invasions, wars, coups, dictators,

00:13:57.470 --> 00:13:58.860
and imposed emperors.

00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:03.970
When Porfirio Diaz finally seizes power in
five minutes’ time, you might wonder why

00:14:03.970 --> 00:14:06.560
no-one goes into rebellion against him.

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:09.790
Why there’s no great war against his dictatorship.

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:11.470
Well, ask yourself this.

00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:16.389
If you’d lived through all that chaos, would
you really be itching for another war?

00:14:16.389 --> 00:14:21.700
Four years after Diaz retired to his ranch,
in 1871, another election rolled around.

00:14:21.700 --> 00:14:27.170
At this point, Benito Juarez had effectively
been in power since La Reforma, a near 15-year

00:14:27.170 --> 00:14:28.170
run.

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:31.959
It was widely expected that he would step
aside to allow some fresh blood.

00:14:31.959 --> 00:14:37.559
Someone like, oh, I dunno, a certain moustachioed
general living on his ranch in Oaxaca?

00:14:37.559 --> 00:14:39.009
But, no.

00:14:39.009 --> 00:14:42.070
Come 1871, Juarez stood for reelection.

00:14:42.070 --> 00:14:44.470
It was at this point that Diaz snapped.

00:14:44.470 --> 00:14:48.649
Back in 1867, Diaz had been deferential to
his former patron.

00:14:48.649 --> 00:14:53.509
This election, the gloves weren’t just off;
they’d been filled with dynamite and shoved

00:14:53.509 --> 00:14:54.900
up Juarez’s ass.

00:14:54.900 --> 00:15:00.130
Diaz ran on a platform relentlessly attacking
Juarez as a dictator in the making, as another

00:15:00.130 --> 00:15:01.130
Santa Anna.

00:15:01.130 --> 00:15:05.459
He made his rallying cry the phrase “no
reelection!”, and made limiting presidents

00:15:05.459 --> 00:15:07.829
to a single term a key part of his pledge.

00:15:07.829 --> 00:15:12.639
When Juarez still squeaked to victory, Diaz
jumped on his horse, picked up his gun, and

00:15:12.639 --> 00:15:15.809
rode back off into the mountains to start
another rebellion.

00:15:15.809 --> 00:15:18.170
Only, it didn’t quite work.

00:15:18.170 --> 00:15:23.110
While there were no real battles, the few
skirmishes Diaz’s men participated in resulted

00:15:23.110 --> 00:15:25.449
in the army wiping the floor with them.

00:15:25.449 --> 00:15:30.410
It’s entirely likely Diaz would’ve just
fizzled out, had fate not intervened.

00:15:30.410 --> 00:15:35.569
On July 18, 1872, Benito Juarez suffered a
heart attack and died at his desk.

00:15:35.569 --> 00:15:40.410
With Juarez gone, a dude called Sebastián
Lerdo de Tejada took over as president.

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:43.930
Luckily, Lerdo was a guy who was all about
burying the hatchet.

00:15:43.930 --> 00:15:48.730
That summer, Lerdo issued an amnesty for everyone
who’d taken up arms against Juarez.

00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:53.810
Diaz returned from the mountains, presumably
thinking the next election was in the bag.

00:15:53.810 --> 00:15:58.550
And then 1875 rolled around and Lerdo decided
to stand for reelection.

00:15:58.550 --> 00:16:03.010
One of the major failures of Diaz’s anti-Juarez
rebellion had been a lack of funds.

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:08.509
So, this time, Diaz didn’t instantly declare
himself in rebellion, but traveled to the

00:16:08.509 --> 00:16:09.509
USA.

00:16:09.509 --> 00:16:13.069
There, he arranged some meetings with some
very rich businessmen, and let it be known

00:16:13.069 --> 00:16:18.470
- very subtly - that, were they to help finance
his bid for the top job, he might just start

00:16:18.470 --> 00:16:21.660
opening up Mexico to wealthy American investors.

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:27.089
Funding secured, Diaz then declared himself
in rebellion under his old “no reelection!”

00:16:27.089 --> 00:16:28.089
slogan.

00:16:28.089 --> 00:16:31.589
Tooled up with American guns and money, Diaz’s
army was now unstoppable.

00:16:31.589 --> 00:16:34.589
But there would be no major battle.

00:16:34.589 --> 00:16:38.120
When Lerdo saw which way the wind was blowing
he was all like “you know what?

00:16:38.120 --> 00:16:40.309
You can have the presidency.”

00:16:40.309 --> 00:16:43.620
Porfirio Diaz finally became president of
Mexico in early 1877.

00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:48.800
One of his first acts was to alter the constitution
to make reelection illegal.

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:53.369
But while people in 1870s Mexico might’ve
thought this proved Diaz was for real with

00:16:53.369 --> 00:16:59.149
his slogan, from our vantage point in the
21st Century, we can see just how hollow that

00:16:59.149 --> 00:17:00.540
promise was.

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:03.950
Porfirio Diaz was now in power, and he had
no intention of giving it up.

00:17:03.950 --> 00:17:08.529
Mexico would be under his perfumed boot for
the next thirty six years.

00:17:08.529 --> 00:17:10.720
The Porfiriato

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:20.419
If you’ve seen Narcos, you’ll recognize
Pablo Escobar’s phrase: plata o plomo, meaning

00:17:20.419 --> 00:17:21.760
“silver or lead”.

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:25.150
I.e. work with me and get rich, work against
me and get dead.

00:17:25.150 --> 00:17:27.670
Well, Porfirio Diaz had his own version.

00:17:27.670 --> 00:17:31.840
The period of Diaz’s rule is known today
as the Porfiriato, and its unofficial slogan

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:36.090
was pan o palo, or “bread or stick.”

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:41.090
The bread part was for Diaz’s old enemies,
or others who might have the power to challenge

00:17:41.090 --> 00:17:42.090
him.

00:17:42.090 --> 00:17:46.090
These guys got not only showered with money,
but guarantees they could run their power

00:17:46.090 --> 00:17:52.720
bases like personal fiefdoms, in return for
not getting any ideas about being president.

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:55.110
The stick part… well, that was for everyone
else.

00:17:55.110 --> 00:17:59.970
Peasants who objected to their crops being
taken by greedy elites; indigenous villagers

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:05.441
worried about haciendas stealing their land;
workers who wanted to unionize… they all

00:18:05.441 --> 00:18:08.120
got whacked with the Porfiriato’s stick.

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:09.690
Not that this meant they were killed.

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:14.190
Murder wasn’t Diaz’s style, he was too
refined for that.

00:18:14.190 --> 00:18:18.100
Nah, what it meant was suddenly having all
your property confiscated.

00:18:18.100 --> 00:18:22.990
Or being deported to do backbreaking labor
in some far-flung region without your family

00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:24.559
knowing where you were.

00:18:24.559 --> 00:18:28.540
Murder and torture aside, Diaz didn’t really
care what happened to you.

00:18:28.540 --> 00:18:31.460
That’s because he had a bigger project to
focus on.

00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:34.360
Diaz wanted to make Mexico great again.

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:36.070
There were two parts to his plan.

00:18:36.070 --> 00:18:40.029
First, Diaz needed to ensure the chaos of
the last fifty years was over.

00:18:40.029 --> 00:18:43.710
So he sent waves of police and vigilantes
out into the provinces, where he let them

00:18:43.710 --> 00:18:47.460
take care of the bandits and highwaymen in
whatever way they saw fit.

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:52.200
He had troublesome villages broken up, their
members all separately shipped off to do hard

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:53.200
labor.

00:18:53.200 --> 00:18:57.159
That done, Diaz turned his attention to the
second part: attract money into Mexico.

00:18:57.159 --> 00:19:02.059
At the start of the Porfiriato, Mexico was
deeply in debt, with a poor international

00:19:02.059 --> 00:19:03.059
reputation.

00:19:03.059 --> 00:19:04.909
Diaz made sure all of that changed.

00:19:04.909 --> 00:19:06.830
He made debt repayments a priority.

00:19:06.830 --> 00:19:11.470
Then, once people accepted that money invested
in Mexico wasn’t money lost, he went back

00:19:11.470 --> 00:19:16.390
up north to see his American business pals
and present them with his grand vision.

00:19:16.390 --> 00:19:21.549
They would invest in Mexico, and Diaz would
ensure they had monopolies, and that any troublesome

00:19:21.549 --> 00:19:25.730
workers were dealt with using the Porfirian
stick.

00:19:25.730 --> 00:19:28.429
In return, these investors would modernize
his country.

00:19:28.429 --> 00:19:32.690
After all its warring, Mexico had almost missed
out on the industrial revolution.

00:19:32.690 --> 00:19:37.090
There were few rail lines, and some provinces
were still so isolated that they were effectively

00:19:37.090 --> 00:19:38.090
self-governing.

00:19:38.090 --> 00:19:39.309
But not anymore.

00:19:39.309 --> 00:19:44.130
Almost overnight, railroad tracks sprang up,
linking cities to ports, to the USA.

00:19:44.130 --> 00:19:48.220
Telegraph poles marched out by the thousands,
connecting villages for the first time to

00:19:48.220 --> 00:19:49.220
the modern world.

00:19:49.220 --> 00:19:52.840
Mines opened, digging up silver, zinc and
copper by the bucketload.

00:19:52.840 --> 00:19:56.960
All this created one of the most sustained
economic booms on record.

00:19:56.960 --> 00:20:00.309
Money was suddenly pouring into a Mexico that
was suddenly safe.

00:20:00.309 --> 00:20:02.120
What was there not to love?

00:20:02.120 --> 00:20:06.950
Diaz had gambled that, after a half century
of mayhem, Mexicans would accept him trampling

00:20:06.950 --> 00:20:11.500
civil liberties in return for law and order
and a chance to get rich.

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:12.500
And he was right.

00:20:12.500 --> 00:20:16.679
The reason no-one rebelled against the Porfiriato
was because no-one wanted to.

00:20:16.679 --> 00:20:19.560
But Diaz’s boom wasn’t evenly distributed.

00:20:19.560 --> 00:20:24.580
While the elites, the foreign investors, and
the new middle class all got fat on Porfirian

00:20:24.580 --> 00:20:30.179
bread; millions of peasants, indigenous, and
urban poor got nothing but the stick.

00:20:30.179 --> 00:20:31.860
Then there was the hypocrisy.

00:20:31.860 --> 00:20:36.429
In 1880, Diaz honored his pledge and stepped
down, refusing to run for reelection.

00:20:36.429 --> 00:20:40.700
Instead, he installed a puppet president,
and made sure this puppet passed just enough

00:20:40.700 --> 00:20:45.820
unpopular laws that, come 1884, the people
were begging Diaz to return.

00:20:45.820 --> 00:20:47.750
So Diaz did as his people asked.

00:20:47.750 --> 00:20:51.820
And then he amended the constitution to make
it legal for the president to have unlimited

00:20:51.820 --> 00:20:52.820
terms.

00:20:52.820 --> 00:20:55.679
So much for “no reelection.”

00:20:55.679 --> 00:20:58.690
The Creelman Interview

00:20:58.690 --> 00:21:05.760
By 1908, the Porfiriato was less a dictatorship
than it was a fact of life.

00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:11.059
Diaz was now the longest-serving ruler in
Mexican history, making even Santa Anna look

00:21:11.059 --> 00:21:12.460
a novice.

00:21:12.460 --> 00:21:16.140
As the Porfiriato had ossified, so too had
the country.

00:21:16.140 --> 00:21:19.960
Democracy had been dead so long that no-one
could remember what it looked like.

00:21:19.960 --> 00:21:21.720
Land rights were a thing of the past.

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:24.630
The rich kept getting richer, and the poor
kept getting poorer.

00:21:24.630 --> 00:21:30.029
It was against this backdrop of stagnation
and resentment that the 77-year old Diaz gave

00:21:30.029 --> 00:21:32.350
the interview that would destroy him.

00:21:32.350 --> 00:21:36.669
The Creelman interview - by American journalist
James Creelman - was meant to be a puff piece.

00:21:36.669 --> 00:21:40.570
It’s certainly written that way: its portrait
of Diaz is so sugar-coated that it should

00:21:40.570 --> 00:21:43.390
carry a diabetic health warning.

00:21:43.390 --> 00:21:48.120
But deep within Creelman’s sycophantic prose
was buried a landmine.

00:21:48.120 --> 00:21:52.070
One that, when it detonated, would blow up
the whole of Mexico.

00:21:52.070 --> 00:21:55.590
Amazingly, it was planted there by Diaz himself.

00:21:55.590 --> 00:22:00.330
“I have waited patiently for the day when
the people of the Mexican Republic would be

00:22:00.330 --> 00:22:04.600
prepared to choose and change their government
at every election…” he was quoted as saying.

00:22:04.600 --> 00:22:06.600
“I believe that day has come.”

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:11.679
The words were vague, meant to reassure readers
in the democracy-loving US that Diaz wasn’t

00:22:11.679 --> 00:22:12.890
a bad guy.

00:22:12.890 --> 00:22:16.540
But they also gave everyone in Mexico the
impression that Diaz wouldn’t stand in the

00:22:16.540 --> 00:22:17.540
1910 election.

00:22:17.540 --> 00:22:21.140
You know that phrase: “when you’re in
a hole, stop digging?”

00:22:21.140 --> 00:22:26.010
Well Porfirio Diaz was evidently of the “better
to dig straight through to Australia” school.

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:29.200
When everyone asked if he really was gonna
stand aside and let another president take

00:22:29.200 --> 00:22:33.529
his place, he was all like, “sure, why not?”

00:22:33.529 --> 00:22:37.450
He stood by this all through 1909, as new
political parties emerged.

00:22:37.450 --> 00:22:43.380
As a pro-democracy businessman named Francisco
I. Madero declared he would run under Diaz’s

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:45.730
former slogan of “no reelection!”

00:22:45.730 --> 00:22:50.279
Diaz even stood by his words as Madero began
campaigning in 1910, drawing enormous crowds

00:22:50.279 --> 00:22:51.799
of cheering supporters.

00:22:51.799 --> 00:22:56.140
Maybe Diaz was so blinkered that he assumed
he’d win in a fair fight.

00:22:56.140 --> 00:23:01.350
Maybe he really did want democracy in 1908,
then got cold feet as the election approached.

00:23:01.350 --> 00:23:03.950
Either way, the result was the same.

00:23:03.950 --> 00:23:06.760
Right before the election, Francisco Madero
was arrested.

00:23:06.760 --> 00:23:12.130
On polling day, everyone was given a choice:
vote for Diaz, or get a taste of the ol’

00:23:12.130 --> 00:23:13.130
Porfirian stick.

00:23:13.130 --> 00:23:18.190
But you can’t let a genie like democracy
out of its bottle and hope to just stuff it

00:23:18.190 --> 00:23:19.190
back in.

00:23:19.190 --> 00:23:24.299
Diaz had given Mexico something unbelievably
dangerous, as dangerous as handing the electorate

00:23:24.299 --> 00:23:28.380
a loaded gun and placing the barrel against
his forehead.

00:23:28.380 --> 00:23:30.299
He’d given them hope.

00:23:30.299 --> 00:23:35.200
On October 5, 1910, Francisco Madero escaped
jail and fled into exile in Texas.

00:23:35.200 --> 00:23:40.850
There, he issued the Plan of San Luis de Potosí,
calling on the Mexican people to rise up and

00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:41.850
overthrow Diaz.

00:23:41.850 --> 00:23:45.510
At long, long last, the Mexican Revolution
was here.

00:23:45.510 --> 00:23:49.540
By the time it ended, no-one would be left
standing at all.

00:23:49.540 --> 00:23:54.799
The End of Order

00:23:54.799 --> 00:23:59.580
You might already know that November 20 is
celebrated as Mexico’s day of revolution.

00:23:59.580 --> 00:24:04.210
What you might not know is that, had you been
alive on November 20, 1910, you wouldn’t

00:24:04.210 --> 00:24:06.039
have realized anything was happening.

00:24:06.039 --> 00:24:09.970
That’s because almost nobody in Mexico heeded
Francisco Madero’s call.

00:24:09.970 --> 00:24:13.630
In the capital, the Porfiriato grinded on
like nothing had changed.

00:24:13.630 --> 00:24:16.730
But “almost nobody” is not the same as
“nobody”.

00:24:16.730 --> 00:24:21.799
Up in the north, a guy called Pancho Villa
decided to follow Madero and get his revolution

00:24:21.799 --> 00:24:22.799
on.

00:24:22.799 --> 00:24:27.910
What began as a little bit of disorder quickly
snowballed into an all-consuming avalanche.

00:24:27.910 --> 00:24:32.320
The Army had got fat and lazy on Porfirian
bread and forgotten how to fight.

00:24:32.320 --> 00:24:35.850
Villa and his armies spent the winter racking
up victories.

00:24:35.850 --> 00:24:39.720
By mid-February, Francisco Madero was back
in Mexico, ready to lead the revolt.

00:24:39.720 --> 00:24:43.039
Yet, even now, Diaz couldn’t see the danger
he was in.

00:24:43.039 --> 00:24:46.260
He remained blind to it as winter gave way
to spring.

00:24:46.260 --> 00:24:49.940
As Emiliano Zapata’s rebellion broke out
in Morelos.

00:24:49.940 --> 00:24:53.970
Even as Villa’s army laid siege to Ciudad
Juarez.

00:24:53.970 --> 00:24:59.640
It wasn’t until the city was about to fall
that Diaz seems to have woken up to the looming

00:24:59.640 --> 00:25:00.640
threat.

00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:02.500
But by then it was too late.

00:25:02.500 --> 00:25:04.529
In early May, Ciudad Juarez fell.

00:25:04.529 --> 00:25:08.110
Around the same time, the Zapatistas captured
the city of Cuautla.

00:25:08.110 --> 00:25:10.100
There were now only two options.

00:25:10.100 --> 00:25:14.799
Diaz either resigned, or he dug his heels
in and plunged Mexico into a bloody civil

00:25:14.799 --> 00:25:15.799
war.

00:25:15.799 --> 00:25:19.980
But Diaz wasn’t a dictator like Syria’s
Assad, ready to drown his country in blood

00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:20.980
to cling to power.

00:25:20.980 --> 00:25:22.570
He was a gentleman.

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:24.630
And gentlemen knew when to quit.

00:25:24.630 --> 00:25:30.149
On May 21, 1911, the treaty of Ciudad Juarez
was signed, paving the way for an interim

00:25:30.149 --> 00:25:32.149
president and new elections.

00:25:32.149 --> 00:25:36.830
Four days later, on May 25, Porfirio Diaz
resigned the presidency and boarded a boat

00:25:36.830 --> 00:25:37.830
to Europe.

00:25:37.830 --> 00:25:40.399
After 36 years, the Porfiriato was over.

00:25:40.399 --> 00:25:45.809
As he left, Diaz famously declared of the
revolution: “Madero has unleashed a tiger,

00:25:45.809 --> 00:25:49.280
now let us see if he can control it.”

00:25:49.280 --> 00:25:51.850
Spoiler alert: he couldn’t.

00:25:51.850 --> 00:25:56.159
In the next two years, Francisco Madero would
first become president, then be assassinated

00:25:56.159 --> 00:25:58.090
by General Victoriano Huerta.

00:25:58.090 --> 00:26:03.169
Huerta’s coup would be what turned the Mexican
Revolution into an inferno, sparking the violence

00:26:03.169 --> 00:26:06.270
that killed hundreds of thousands.

00:26:06.270 --> 00:26:09.110
But all that is a story for another time.

00:26:09.110 --> 00:26:13.550
Porfirio Diaz spent the last four years of
his life in exile in Paris, watching his former

00:26:13.550 --> 00:26:15.270
country disintegrate.

00:26:15.270 --> 00:26:20.860
Possibly with sadness, possibly with a feeling
of satisfaction that he’d been right all

00:26:20.860 --> 00:26:23.720
along: Mexico really was ungovernable.

00:26:23.720 --> 00:26:26.580
Diaz finally died on July 2, 1915.

00:26:26.580 --> 00:26:28.840
He was buried in Montparnasse Cemetery.

00:26:28.840 --> 00:26:32.029
Today, Porfirio Diaz’s reputation is still
unsettled.

00:26:32.029 --> 00:26:35.450
On the one hand, there are those who point
to the stability he brought.

00:26:35.450 --> 00:26:38.649
To the real economic progress made under his
watch.

00:26:38.649 --> 00:26:43.030
On the other, there are those who point to
the millions left behind by the Porfiriato.

00:26:43.030 --> 00:26:49.700
The millions abandoned until they got so frustrated
that violent revolution was the only option.

00:26:49.700 --> 00:26:51.850
Maybe both sides have a point.

00:26:51.850 --> 00:26:54.539
Without a doubt, Porfirio Diaz was a dictator.

00:26:54.539 --> 00:26:59.570
Without a doubt, he squandered the security
and progress his regime brought by showering

00:26:59.570 --> 00:27:01.350
money on a narrow elite.

00:27:01.350 --> 00:27:05.870
But Diaz was also the lynchpin of an entire
era of Mexican history.

00:27:05.870 --> 00:27:09.419
A guy who ruled for longer than anyone else.

00:27:09.419 --> 00:27:13.320
His story may not be a pretty one, or even
an inspiring one.

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:14.970
But it’s one we need to remember.

00:27:14.970 --> 00:27:21.140
For better or for worse, without Porfirio
Diaz, modern Mexico would be a very different

00:27:21.140 --> 00:27:38.789
place.

